# MNIST Classification Example — MATLAB

Classify handwritten MNIST digits with a linear SVM using LIBSVM's MATLAB interface.

[GPL-3.0 license](LICENSE)

## Quick Start

1. Open the repository directory in MATLAB.
2. Configure a supported C++ compiler and run the example:

```matlab
mex -setup c++
classify_MNIST
```

The script decompresses the four included MNIST files, extracts `mnistHelper.zip` and `libsvm-master.zip`, builds LIBSVM's MEX interface, and trains and evaluates a linear SVM. Keep the input archives beside the script.

## Historical Environment

The original documentation records MATLAB R2015b, Microsoft Windows SDK 7.1 / Visual C++ 2012, LIBSVM 3.22, and mnistHelper dated 28 April 2011. It reports 93.98% test accuracy; this is the original result, not a newly reproduced benchmark.

## Repository Structure

- [classify_MNIST.m](classify_MNIST.m): preparation, training, and evaluation.
- [mnistHelper.zip](mnistHelper.zip): MNIST image and label readers.
- [libsvm-master.zip](libsvm-master.zip): LIBSVM sources.
- `train-images-idx3-ubyte.gz` and `train-labels-idx1-ubyte.gz`: training inputs.
- `t10k-images-idx3-ubyte.gz` and `t10k-labels-idx1-ubyte.gz`: test inputs.

## Related Projects

- [LIBSVM](https://github.com/cjlin1/libsvm): linear SVM implementation used by the example.
- [Stanford MNIST helper documentation](http://ufldl.stanford.edu/wiki/index.php/Using_the_MNIST_Dataset): original source linked for the helper functions.

